For the employment outcome analysis, I initially used LASSO logistic regression to identify the important predictors.
After that, I refitted a normal logistic regression model using the selected variables and carried out likelihood ratio tests to remove variables that did not significantly improve the model.
I also tested some possible interaction effects
LASSO was chosen because it gave slightly better predictive performance and helped identify the variables that contributed most to prediction.
However, after refitting the logistic regression and performing the hypothesis tests, the final model ended up being very similar to the model selected using AIC.
For the salary analysis, I compared LASSO regression and an AIC-selected linear regression model. 
LASSO achieved a slightly lower MSE, so it performed better for prediction.
However, because the research question is about identifying factors that determine salary levels, I'm considering using the AIC-selected model for interpretation.
should I also have used the AIC approach for the employment determinant analysis, since that question is also about identifying important factors rather than only prediction.
Two approached:
Use LASSO as a variable selection method, then refit a standard regression model for interpretation, for both employment and salary; or
Use AIC-selected models for both since the aim is to understand the determinants.


